README

This sample demonstrates the software reset library in addon/softreset. The
Raspberry Pi can be rebooted from three different sources:

1. Keyboard: Press Ctrl+Alt+Del on an USB keyboard. The keyboard can be
   attached at any time (USB plug-and-play is enabled).

2. GPIO: Connect a push button between GPIO17 (header pin 11) and GND. The
   internal pull-up resistor is enabled. Hold the button for 2 seconds to reboot.
   The input must have been released once after boot, before it can trigger a
   reset, so that a stuck button does not lead to a reset loop.

3. Ethernet: Send the UDP command "REBOOT circle" to port 5050. The IP address
   is obtained using DHCP and displayed on the screen. You can use the included
   Python 3 script on your host computer:

	python3 softreset.py 192.168.0.123 circle

   The script prints "OK" when the command has been accepted. You can check if
   the system is alive with:

	python3 softreset.py 192.168.0.123 --ping

   It is also possible to use netcat:

	echo "REBOOT circle" | nc -u -w1 192.168.0.123 5050

Before the system reboots, a reset handler of the application is called (here
it only switches off the ACT LED), which can be used to bring the application
into a safe state. The GPIO pin, hold time, UDP port and password can be
modified at the top of kernel.cpp.

The addon/softreset library has to be built first:

	cd addon/softreset
	make

Then build this sample:

	cd sample/53-softreset
	make

Please note that the network reset command is not encrypted. Use it in trusted
networks only.
